# Cloudflare Pages reads this file from the root of the build output.
#
# The bare root is not a language, so it goes to the default one, which is
# ENGLISH from 2026-08-02 (DESIGN-PLAN section 37). Astro's own
# static redirect emits an index.html carrying a meta refresh, which works but
# answers 200 and costs the reader a round trip. Cloudflare applies redirects
# before static assets, so this turns the same hop into a real 301. The Astro
# page stays underneath as a fallback and is what the dev server uses.
#
# This is a path redirect, not language detection: nothing here reads
# Accept-Language, and the switch stays visible on every page.
/  /en  301

# The pieces are Creature, the owner's own word for them (DESIGN-PLAN section
# 22), so /pieces/ became /creature/ on 2026-08-02. Nothing had been shared and
# the site is noindexed, so nothing is known to break; these exist because a URL
# that once resolved should not start answering 404, and they cost nothing.
# The archive became a GALLERY on 2026-08-03 (DESIGN-PLAN section 18): it holds
# the brand's imagery as a sequence, not a dated record, so the label and the
# path now say the same thing.
# The gallery dissolved into the process page on 2026-08-04 (DESIGN-PLAN
# section 65): imagery has one home now. The old /archive hops point straight
# at /process so no reader crosses two redirects.
/it/archive  /it/process  301
/en/archive  /en/process  301
/it/gallery  /it/process  301
/en/gallery  /en/process  301

# The designer folded into the end of the about page on 2026-08-03: two
# sections telling one story, so they are one page now.
/it/designer  /it/about  301
/en/designer  /en/about  301

# RUBEDO CAME BACK, and its redirect had to go with it (2026-08-16).
#
# The red shirt left the catalogue on 2026-08-04 (DESIGN-PLAN section 50) and
# these two lines sent its old page to /process, where the piece had gone. It
# is a Creature again — eleven photographs, a page that builds, a tile in the
# worn band — and the redirect stayed, which made it the one broken link on
# the site: Cloudflare applies redirects BEFORE static assets, so
# /en/creature/rubedo answered /en/process while /en/creature/rubedo/ answered
# the piece. Every internal link is written without the trailing slash, so
# every route to Rubedo went to the process page and nothing else did.
#
# The lesson is the one this file already knew, inverted: a redirect costs
# nothing while the destination is right, and shadows a real page the moment
# the thing comes back. Anything added here has to be removed when it does.

# The enquiry became the order on 2026-08-04: same flow, named for what it is.
# Severya took its own name on 2026-08-04, out of the sample-sale folder.
/it/creature/capo-08  /it/creature/severya  301
/en/creature/capo-08  /en/creature/severya  301

# The two Monumentus Vests were one vest (2026-08-16, the owner). capo-04 held
# the same name, collection, price, stage and wornBy as capo-03 and one
# photograph the other did not; its frame moved across and its page went.
#
# Unlike the Rubedo rule above, this one is safe to leave: capo-04 is not a
# piece that could come back, because it was never a piece. If that ever stops
# being true, these two lines have to go the same day.
/it/creature/capo-04  /it/creature/capo-03  301
/en/creature/capo-04  /en/creature/capo-03  301

# VERTEX IS NOT THIS BRAND'S PIECE (2026-08-20, the owner). The hat belongs to
# another project of his, so it came off the site. It was UNPUBLISHED and not
# deleted: the document is sitting in drafts with its photograph attached, and
# a dataset export was taken first (~/aleksander-cecco-backups/).
#
# Which is exactly why these two lines carry this file's standing warning, and
# carry it more sharply than the capo-04 pair above: capo-04 was never a piece
# and cannot come back, and Vertex CAN — it is one click from being published
# again. Cloudflare answers redirects before static assets, so the day it
# returns these two lines shadow its page and every link to it lands on the
# catalogue instead. That is the Rubedo failure, and it took a session to find.
#
# THE DAY VERTEX COMES BACK, THESE TWO LINES GO. Same commit, not the next one.
/it/creature/capo-14  /it/creature  301
/en/creature/capo-14  /en/creature  301

/it/creature/:slug/enquiry  /it/creature/:slug/order  301
/en/creature/:slug/enquiry  /en/creature/:slug/order  301

/it/pieces/*  /it/creature/:splat  301
/en/pieces/*  /en/creature/:splat  301

# THE ORDER PAGE BECAME THE CART (2026-08-16, DESIGN-PLAN section 129). The
# owner saw the page that listed every piece at once and did not want it; the
# cart replaces it and the page is deleted.
#
# These two lines are for HIM. The page existed for a day, the site is
# noindexed, and nobody else can have the URL — but he was sent it to look at,
# so a tab he still has open lands on the thing that replaced it rather than on
# the 404. **They carry the shadowing risk this file records above**: the day
# anything is built at /[lang]/order these lines answer first and it will look
# like the page does not exist. There is no plan to build one.
/it/order  /it/cart  301
/en/order  /en/cart  301
